<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacques-Joseph Fabry</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1722">1722</a>–<a href="/years/1798">1798</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jacques-Joseph Fabry was a politician of the Prince-Bishopric of Liège and a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liege-revolution">Liège Revolution</a> of 1789. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-remy-de-chestret">Jean-Rémy de Chestret</a> he headed the patriot government that took control of the city after the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> fled, proclaiming the rights of citizens. After Austrian troops restored the prince-bishop in 1791 he went into exile, returning with the French, who annexed Liège in 1795.</p></div>
